                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Radio maps (RMs) characterize the spatial distribution of wireless signal attributes, such as received signal strength (RSS), signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), and power spectral density (PSD). They provide radio-environment information for spectrum management, interference monitoring, base-station planning, localization, and adaptive network optimization. RMs are also relevant to wireless digital twins and integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), where propagation knowledge supports both communication and environmental awareness [2]–[6]. This paper focuses on RM reconstruction from limited RSS measurements and investigates how prior propagation knowledge can be incorporated into this task.",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                1149,
                604,
                1245
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    92.0,
                    1149.0
                ],
                [
                    604.0,
                    1149.0
                ],
                [
                    604.0,
                    1245.0
                ],
                [
                    92.0,
                    1245.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "",
            "block_bbox": [
                617,
                398,
                1130,
                614
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    617.0,
                    398.0
                ],
                [
                    1130.0,
                    398.0
                ],
                [
                    1130.0,
                    614.0
                ],
                [
                    617.0,
                    614.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Existing RM reconstruction studies can be roughly divided into two lines. The first line is measurement-free RM generation, which synthesizes complete RMs from environmental priors, such as building layouts, material assumptions, and transmitter (Tx) locations. Classical ray-tracing methods provide physically interpretable predictions based on geometric propagation models [7]. Recent learning-based methods, including U-Net-based generators [8] and diffusion models such as RadioDiff [9], improve generation efficiency by learning propagation patterns from data. Although these methods do not directly solve sparse-measurement-based RM estimation, they provide useful generative priors for radio propagation modeling.",
            "block_bbox": [
                617,
                614,
                1130,
                925
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    617.0,
                    614.0
                ],
                [
                    1130.0,
                    614.0
                ],
                [
                    1130.0,
                    925.0
                ],
                [
                    617.0,
                    925.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The second line directly estimates RMs from sparse RSS measurements. Classical interpolation methods, such as Kriging [10], [11], exploit spatial correlation among samples but are limited in complex environments with blockage, shadowing, and multipath effects. Supervised deep models, including auto-encoder-based methods [12], generative adversarial networks [13], [14], and diffusion-based estimators [15], [16], learn nonlinear mappings from sparse observations to complete RMs. More recently, inverse reconstruction methods have been explored to reduce the need for deployment-time fine-tuning. For example, LaPnP combines measurement consistency with latent-domain plug-and-play denoising [17], while RadioDiff-Inverse formulates sparse-measurement RM reconstruction as a Bayesian inverse problem with a diffusion prior [18].",
            "block_bbox": [
                617,
                925,
                1131,
                1308
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 10,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    617.0,
                    925.0
                ],
                [
                    1131.0,
                    925.0
                ],
                [
                    1131.0,
                    1308.0
                ],
                [
                    617.0,
                    1308.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "footnote",
            "block_content": "A preliminary version of this work was presented at 35th IEEE International Workshop on Machine Learning for Signal Processing (MLSP) [1].",
            "block_bbox": [
                93,
                1262,
                604,
                1317
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": null,
            "group_id": 11,
